WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.146
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:04.146 --> 00:00:05.912
Building Non-Person Identity.

3
00:00:05.912 --> 00:00:10.637
This is Chapter 4: app management
policies and certificate rotation.

4
00:00:10.637 --> 00:00:12.524
The chapter does two jobs.

5
00:00:12.524 --> 00:00:16.845
It holds the tenant's rules for app
credentials in a reviewed file that can be

6
00:00:16.845 --> 00:00:20.479
compared and applied without ever
loosening, and it rotates app

7
00:00:20.479 --> 00:00:24.525
certificates through Key Vault, with
an owner's approval and an overlap.

8
00:00:24.525 --> 00:00:28.470
We'll start from Microsoft's own
samples, see what they leave out,

9
00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:31.125
then walk the code, the
labs and the dangers.

10
00:00:31.125 --> 00:00:36.640
One note before we begin: the code is
illustrative, tested against fixtures.

11
00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:41.902
Why does this chapter matter?

12
00:00:41.902 --> 00:00:44.402
Because both of its subjects fail quietly.

13
00:00:44.402 --> 00:00:47.963
A tenant policy that someone
set once in the portal can't

14
00:00:47.963 --> 00:00:51.602
be reviewed or compared, so
nobody notices when it drifts.

15
00:00:51.602 --> 00:00:56.202
And a certificate that nobody rotates is
simply an outage waiting for its date.

16
00:00:56.202 --> 00:00:59.205
So the chapter turns the
policy into a reviewed file,

17
00:00:59.205 --> 00:01:03.603
compared with the tenant and applied
through a path that can only tighten it.

18
00:01:03.603 --> 00:01:07.046
And it turns rotation into
a plan: raised early,

19
00:01:07.046 --> 00:01:11.940
approved by an owner, renewed,
overlapped, and only then cleaned up.

20
00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:13.780
Let's see where it runs.

21
00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:18.893
Next, which cloud.

22
00:01:18.893 --> 00:01:22.556
This chapter talks to two
services, so it needs two hosts.

23
00:01:22.556 --> 00:01:26.398
The policy and application
calls go to Microsoft Graph,

24
00:01:26.398 --> 00:01:31.493
and for a GCC tenant like this estate's,
that's the worldwide Graph endpoint.

25
00:01:31.493 --> 00:01:36.724
The certificates live in Key Vault, and
because GCC's estate uses commercial Azure,

26
00:01:36.724 --> 00:01:39.692
the vault sits on the
commercial vault domain.

27
00:01:39.692 --> 00:01:42.838
GCC High and DoD have
their own hosts for both,

28
00:01:42.838 --> 00:01:47.854
and every policy page the chapter
calls lists the US Government clouds.

29
00:01:47.854 --> 00:01:52.820
The code never guesses: you pass the
cloud, and it builds both hosts from it.

30
00:01:56.570 --> 00:01:58.395
Before the code, the model.

31
00:01:58.395 --> 00:02:00.783
Learn describes two kinds of policy.

32
00:02:00.783 --> 00:02:04.789
There's one tenant default, which
always exists and starts switched off,

33
00:02:04.789 --> 00:02:07.505
with separate restrictions
for apps that live in

34
00:02:07.505 --> 00:02:10.221
the tenant and apps
provisioned from elsewhere.

35
00:02:10.221 --> 00:02:13.393
And there are app-specific
policies, one per app at most,

36
00:02:13.393 --> 00:02:16.495
which override the default
for whatever they define.

37
00:02:16.495 --> 00:02:20.169
Restrictions only bite on apps
created after a date you choose,

38
00:02:20.169 --> 00:02:23.420
and they never stop an existing
app from getting tokens.

39
00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:25.745
They just refuse the
change that breaks them.

40
00:02:25.745 --> 00:02:30.520
Beta adds more restriction types, but
this chapter stays on version one.

41
00:02:34.270 --> 00:02:38.632
Here are the program's own rules, and
most of them are labeled design guidance.

42
00:02:38.632 --> 00:02:43.017
New apps can't use secrets, and their
certificates are capped at a hundred and

43
00:02:43.017 --> 00:02:45.593
eighty days, set on both
object types because

44
00:02:45.593 --> 00:02:47.820
the admin center expects them to match.

45
00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:50.211
An app that truly can't use a certificate

46
00:02:50.211 --> 00:02:52.820
gets a time-limited
exception with a record.

47
00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:55.182
Older apps get a year while they're fixed.

48
00:02:55.182 --> 00:02:57.832
Every registered app
gets the property lock.

49
00:02:57.832 --> 00:02:59.658
No extra license is needed.

50
00:02:59.658 --> 00:03:03.340
And rotation joins Key Vault,
which can renew a certificate,

51
00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:07.160
to the app registration, which
renews nothing on its own.

52
00:03:10.910 --> 00:03:14.257
Here's what Microsoft gives
you: five Learn sources that,

53
00:03:14.257 --> 00:03:16.585
between them, hold the whole procedure.

54
00:03:16.585 --> 00:03:19.575
There's the request that
turns on the tenant default,

55
00:03:19.575 --> 00:03:24.233
the pair that create and assign an app
policy, Learn's configuration walkthrough,

56
00:03:24.233 --> 00:03:28.335
the request that adds a certificate to
an app, and the Key Vault examples.

57
00:03:28.335 --> 00:03:33.539
Those samples only live inside web pages,
so the repository pins their sources,

58
00:03:33.539 --> 00:03:37.198
unmodified and hashed, with
a weekly watch for changes.

59
00:03:37.198 --> 00:03:42.572
Then a harness sends each request,
exactly as written, to a fake Graph.

60
00:03:42.572 --> 00:03:45.620
That's how you find out
what each one really does.

61
00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:51.612
This is where the samples get interesting,

62
00:03:51.612 --> 00:03:54.558
because run as published,
several of them would hurt you.

63
00:03:54.558 --> 00:03:57.574
The tenant update's start
dates reach back years,

64
00:03:57.574 --> 00:04:01.270
so it would refuse the next
normal certificate on an old app.

65
00:04:01.270 --> 00:04:03.258
Its body isn't even strict.

66
00:04:03.258 --> 00:04:07.558
Learn's own procedure updates only
two restrictions, and since an update

67
00:04:07.558 --> 00:04:12.145
replaces the whole collection, anything
it forgets is silently switched off.

68
00:04:12.145 --> 00:04:14.755
The assignment sample sends a placeholder,

69
00:04:14.755 --> 00:04:17.945
and the tutorial uses a
property version one refuses.

70
00:04:17.945 --> 00:04:23.530
The code keeps the good shape and fixes
each of these, and a test pins every row.

71
00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:29.780
The certificate samples
have a sharper problem.

72
00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:35.039
Learn's first example for adding a
certificate replaces every certificate the app

73
00:04:35.039 --> 00:04:39.917
had, so the old one vanishes the instant
the new one lands, with no overlap at all.

74
00:04:39.917 --> 00:04:44.131
The Key Vault example asks for six
months, which is just over the cap.

75
00:04:44.131 --> 00:04:48.363
And none of the samples ever puts
the renewed certificate on the app,

76
00:04:48.363 --> 00:04:50.630
asks the owner, or removes the old one.

77
00:04:50.630 --> 00:04:55.113
So the verdict is: keep the version
one requests as models for the writes,

78
00:04:55.113 --> 00:04:57.750
and don't run any of them as published.

79
00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:02.825
So what does the code add?

80
00:05:02.825 --> 00:05:08.000
One module, two scripts that only read, and
a single separate script that can write.

81
00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:12.275
What drives it is a handful of small
files, each with a clear keeper.

82
00:05:12.275 --> 00:05:17.542
Identity engineering owns the reviewed
policy file and the rotation settings,

83
00:05:17.542 --> 00:05:20.575
like how early to raise
and how long to overlap.

84
00:05:20.575 --> 00:05:23.462
Application owners keep
three simple records:

85
00:05:23.462 --> 00:05:27.682
which vault holds each app's
certificate, who approved each rotation,

86
00:05:27.682 --> 00:05:31.087
and who confirmed the workload
signs in with the new one.

87
00:05:31.087 --> 00:05:35.640
Every rotation carries one
request ID from start to finish.

88
00:05:39.390 --> 00:05:41.290
Here's how the policy side works.

89
00:05:41.290 --> 00:05:43.604
The code reads the tenant's default,

90
00:05:43.604 --> 00:05:47.515
every app policy and what it's
assigned to, and each app's lock.

91
00:05:47.515 --> 00:05:52.052
Then it compares, restriction by
restriction, on both object types.

92
00:05:52.052 --> 00:05:55.441
Where the tenant is weaker,
it adds the program's rule.

93
00:05:55.441 --> 00:05:57.724
Where the tenant is already stricter,

94
00:05:57.724 --> 00:06:01.628
or holds something the file doesn't
mention, it keeps the tenant's.

95
00:06:01.628 --> 00:06:06.766
That merged body is the key: an update
can never drop or loosen anything.

96
00:06:06.766 --> 00:06:08.442
The plan then turns the diff,

97
00:06:08.442 --> 00:06:12.810
the exceptions and the locks into
actions, and nothing in it writes.

98
00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:19.584
Rotation works the same
way: read everything,

99
00:06:19.584 --> 00:06:22.935
then give each certificate
exactly one next step.

100
00:06:22.935 --> 00:06:26.047
A certificate far from
expiry is left alone.

101
00:06:26.047 --> 00:06:28.497
One that's due waits
for an owner's approval,

102
00:06:28.497 --> 00:06:32.347
and an approval only counts if it's
recent and from an enabled owner.

103
00:06:32.347 --> 00:06:36.236
Approved, it's renewed in Key
Vault using the vault's own policy.

104
00:06:36.236 --> 00:06:41.073
The new certificate is attached beside
the old one, the owner confirms the

105
00:06:41.073 --> 00:06:45.673
workload uses it, a week of overlap
runs, and only then does the old one go.

106
00:06:45.673 --> 00:06:47.560
Secrets aren't rotated at all.

107
00:06:47.560 --> 00:06:49.620
They're flagged for replacement.

108
00:06:53.370 --> 00:06:57.258
Only one script in this chapter can
write, and it's built to say no.

109
00:06:57.258 --> 00:07:01.928
It won't run a plan unless the plan's
hash matches the one a reviewer approved,

110
00:07:01.928 --> 00:07:05.920
and the plan is for this tenant, this
cloud, and less than a week old.

111
00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:10.584
Every request body is checked against
what's allowed: the policy can't loosen,

112
00:07:10.584 --> 00:07:13.027
an attach adds exactly one certificate,

113
00:07:13.027 --> 00:07:16.432
and the Key Vault request can't
change the vault's policy.

114
00:07:16.432 --> 00:07:21.383
Just before each write, it re-reads the
target and skips anything that changed.

115
00:07:21.383 --> 00:07:26.260
And a live run is always a signed-in
person, never an unattended app.

116
00:07:30.010 --> 00:07:31.072
Who is this for?

117
00:07:31.072 --> 00:07:32.061
Four groups.

118
00:07:32.061 --> 00:07:33.447
Developers build it.

119
00:07:33.447 --> 00:07:36.985
Identity engineers run it
and keep the reviewed files.

120
00:07:36.985 --> 00:07:42.373
Application owners approve each rotation
and confirm their workloads still sign in.

121
00:07:42.373 --> 00:07:45.459
And the I S S Os review the evidence.

122
00:07:45.459 --> 00:07:49.850
The permissions are split cleanly:
the reads hold only read permissions,

123
00:07:49.850 --> 00:07:53.435
and anything that writes is
delegated to a signed-in person.

124
00:07:53.435 --> 00:07:56.758
That matters, because an
unattended app with permission to

125
00:07:56.758 --> 00:08:00.285
update every application could
add a credential to all of them.

126
00:08:00.285 --> 00:08:05.610
Microsoft does offer a self-rolling key
option, but it skips the owner's approval.

127
00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:13.522
Lab 1 needs only a laptop,
PowerShell 7 and Pester.

128
00:08:13.522 --> 00:08:17.777
First you send Microsoft's samples to
the fakes, and the tests show what you'd

129
00:08:17.777 --> 00:08:19.149
otherwise take on trust,

130
00:08:19.149 --> 00:08:22.648
including Learn's own update
quietly dropping a restriction.

131
00:08:22.648 --> 00:08:25.586
Then you compare the
policy and build its plan.

132
00:08:25.586 --> 00:08:28.767
It finds six restrictions
to add, one to change,

133
00:08:28.767 --> 00:08:31.697
and one to keep exactly
as the tenant has it.

134
00:08:31.697 --> 00:08:36.847
You dry-run the plan, apply it to the
fake, and then try a hash nobody approved.

135
00:08:36.847 --> 00:08:38.386
The script refuses.

136
00:08:38.386 --> 00:08:40.790
Next, the rotation half.

137
00:08:44.540 --> 00:08:47.902
The rotation plan is where
the design becomes concrete.

138
00:08:47.902 --> 00:08:50.903
Each fictional app is at a
different point in its life.

139
00:08:50.903 --> 00:08:54.827
One is due and freshly approved,
so it's renewed in Key Vault.

140
00:08:54.827 --> 00:08:57.041
One was already renewed by the vault,

141
00:08:57.041 --> 00:09:00.065
so the new certificate is
attached beside the old.

142
00:09:00.065 --> 00:09:04.940
One has been verified and has finished
its overlap, so its old certificate goes.

143
00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:09.190
And three apps still use secrets,
which are flagged for replacement.

144
00:09:09.190 --> 00:09:14.690
Apply it to the fake, run the full suite,
and five hundred thirteen tests pass.

145
00:09:18.440 --> 00:09:22.816
Lab 2 moves to a test tenant and a test
vault, never production, and it's built

146
00:09:22.816 --> 00:09:27.765
to answer the open questions the chapter
couldn't settle from documentation alone.

147
00:09:27.765 --> 00:09:32.212
You read and plan with read-only rights,
keep the policy as it was before,

148
00:09:32.212 --> 00:09:36.890
then apply as a signed-in administrator
and check the admin center agrees.

149
00:09:36.890 --> 00:09:39.140
You try to break the policy on purpose.

150
00:09:39.140 --> 00:09:42.545
Then you carry one certificate
through a full rotation,

151
00:09:42.545 --> 00:09:45.728
recording what Graph and
Key Vault actually return.

152
00:09:45.728 --> 00:09:50.920
Finally, because this code never
loosens a policy, a person backs it out.

153
00:09:54.670 --> 00:09:59.008
Each of these dangers has a guard in
the code, not a reminder in a runbook.

154
00:09:59.008 --> 00:10:03.793
The most dangerous is the quiet one: an
update that forgets a restriction and

155
00:10:03.793 --> 00:10:08.958
switches it off, so the writer re-reads the
live policy and refuses anything looser.

156
00:10:08.958 --> 00:10:13.096
A retroactive start date breaks
rotations on the very day they're due.

157
00:10:13.096 --> 00:10:16.832
A certificate that replaces the
old one takes the workload down.

158
00:10:16.832 --> 00:10:21.408
And an unattended app that can update
every application is a standing risk,

159
00:10:21.408 --> 00:10:23.770
so writes are always a signed-in person.

160
00:10:23.770 --> 00:10:27.440
Notice the pattern: each
guard runs every time.

161
00:10:31.190 --> 00:10:32.540
Here's what done looks like.

162
00:10:32.540 --> 00:10:36.637
On the policy side: a reviewed file
with an approved effective date,

163
00:10:36.637 --> 00:10:41.554
the tenant default matching it on both
object types with no warning in the admin

164
00:10:41.554 --> 00:10:43.938
center, every app policy accounted for,

165
00:10:43.938 --> 00:10:47.365
every exception dated, and
every registered app locked.

166
00:10:47.365 --> 00:10:50.702
On the rotation side: every
certificate bound to a vault,

167
00:10:50.702 --> 00:10:55.707
every rotation approved and overlapped,
every plan approved by someone other than

168
00:10:55.707 --> 00:11:00.277
the person who applied it, and the pinned
samples still the ones you reviewed.

169
00:11:00.277 --> 00:11:02.754
Next, Chapter 5 replaces those flagged

170
00:11:02.754 --> 00:11:05.927
secrets with federation
and managed identities.

171
00:11:05.927 --> 00:11:07.090
Thanks for watching.
